 take half  the difference now certainly the  solution depends on the values of B 1  and B 2 I'm not saying you don't change  the answers by changing the constants on  this side what I am saying is that the  structure by which you find the answers  does not depend on B 1 and B 2 it's  determined solely by the coefficients of  x and Y in other words what we're saying  is no matter what B 1 and B 2 are in  this particular problem to find x and y  we take half the sum of the B's and we  take half the difference in other words  the solution depends on B 1 and B 2 and  then numerically but not structurally  well the whole idea is this and this is  what we so often do in math  Malick's because the solution to our  equations depends on the coefficients of  the X's we somehow or other want to  focus our attention on the coefficients  and we don't need the X's in there  because we can sort of think of the X's  as being a place value type of situation  in other words x1 can be thought of as  being the first column X 2 the second  column the first equation can be thought  of as the first row the second equation  the second row and what this motivates  is a concept called an M by n matrix now  this sounds like a very ominous term an  M by n matrix but the point is it's not  a very ominous term it's in fact I think  that it's to that the word matrix  essentially mean indicates an array and  that's all this thing is by an M by n  matrix we simply mean a rectangular  array of numbers arranged to form M rows  in other words the first member tells  you the number of rows and the second  member tells you the number of columns  now there's certainly nothing logical  about that in terms of our game idea  just memorize this it's a rule of the  game or a definition somebody could have  said why didn't you give the columns  first and then the rows oh we could have  but one of them had to come first and  the convention is is that one refers to  the rows first and then the columns an M  by 